    "https://www.governmentjobs.com/careers/loyno/jobs/5485111/westerfield-fellow": {
      "title": "Westerfield Fellow",
      "description": "Westerfield Fellow Salary Depends on Qualifications Location Broadway Campus, New Orleans, LA Job Type 9 Month Faculty Job Number 202200697 Division Academic Affairs Department College of Law Opening Date 09/16/2026 FLSA Exempt Description Benefits Description Loyola University New Orleans College of Law is accepting applications for our Westerfield Fellowship Program. The fellowship is designed for individuals pursuing a career in law teaching. Fellows have student teaching assistants and a faculty mentor, and they may hire a research assistant. One-year contracts may be renewed, and typical fellowships are two years. Westerfield Fellows have been successful in obtaining tenure track positions at ABA-accredited law schools teaching in many different areas of law. The College of Law is located in one of the most culturally diverse cities in the United States, with unique cuisine, museums, historical sites, and a flourishing arts and music community. New Orleans is the seat of the United States Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals, United States District Court for the Eastern District of Louisiana, Louisiana Supreme Court, Louisiana Fourth Circuit Court of Appeal, as well as other lower courts. The College of Law has a student population of approximately 600 students, over forty faculty members, active clinics that have spearheaded numerous social justice reform efforts, and summer abroad programs. Its location in Louisiana, one of the world’s best known “mixed jurisdictions,” provides unique opportunities for comparative and international law scholarship. Loyola University is an educational institution dedicated to fostering intellectual achievement, personal development, and social responsibility, and it is committed to the human dignity and worth of every person. Examples of Duties Fellows are responsible for teaching two sections of a three-credit legal research and writing course in the fall and an appellate advocacy course in the spring. Fellows teach these courses with the support of an experienced director in a program in which the director and professors coordinate the content and pace of the classes. Fellows also have the opportunity to create and teach a seminar on a topic of their interest in the second year. Complete applications include a cover letter and CV. All applications must be submitted electronically. Typical Qualifications Minimum Juris Doctorate Degree from an accredited United States institution. Foreign-educated candidates with a PhD may be considered. Applicants should have strong academic credentials and excellent communication skills. Physical Requirements: Ability to perform job duties with or without reasonable accommodations. In accordance with the Campus Security Act as amended, Loyola University publishes annually and distributes to members of the academic community, and upon request to prospective (students/employees), an annual security report . That report includes current policies and procedures of the University Police Department for the reporting of campus crimes, notifying the campus community of occurrence of campus crimes, policies regarding access to campus buildings, the enforcement authority of the University Police Officers and their relationship to local police. University policies on the possession, use and sale of alcohol and illegal drugs are outlined. The Campus sexual Assault Program and policy is described. Campus crime statistics as defined under this law for the last three calendar years are included. A copy of this report may be secured from the (Office of Admissions), (Office of Human Resources), or from the University Police Department. Loyola University New Orleans does not unlawfully discriminate on the basis of race, color, gender, sexual orientation, age, religion, disability, veteran's status or national origin in its educational programs or activities, including employment and admissions. At the same time, Loyola cherishes its right to seek and retain personnel who will make a positive contribution to its religious character, goals, and mission in order to enhance the Jesuit, Catholic tradition. We offer a complete benefits package to full-time employees including health care, dental, vision, retirement, flexible spending accounts, life insurance, long-term disability, tuition remission, holidays, vacation, and sick leave. Part-time employees who work an average of 30 hours per week over the course of a year, receive certain benefits on a pro-rated basis. To learn more details, visit our benefits page . Employer Loyola University New Orleans Apply"
